{
  "$schema": "https://json.schemastore.org/sarif-2.1.0.json",
  "version": "2.1.0",
  "runs": [
    {
      "tool": {
        "driver": {
          "name": "aegis",
          "version": "1.0.0",
          "informationUri": "https://github.com/aegis/engine",
          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-007",
              "name": "RDS instance without storage encryption",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "RDS instance without storage encryption"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "RDS storage should be encrypted at rest. The provider default for storage_encrypted is false, so an absent attribute is a finding.\n\nHow to fix:\nEnable storage encryption on the RDS instance:\nresource \"aws_db_instance\" \"this\" {\n  # ...\n  storage_encrypted = true\n  kms_key_id        = aws_kms_key.rds.arn  # optional CMK\n}\nNote: encryption cannot be enabled on an existing unencrypted instance —\ntake a snapshot, copy it with encryption, and restore."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/Overview.Encryption.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-008",
              "name": "RDS instance without deletion protection",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "RDS instance without deletion protection"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Deletion protection guards against accidental database destruction. The provider default is false.\n\nHow to fix:\nEnable deletion protection to prevent accidental destruction:\nresource \"aws_db_instance\" \"this\" {\n  deletion_protection = true\n}\nFor S3, remove `force_destroy = true`. For EC2, set `disable_api_termination = true`."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/USER_DeleteInstance.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "medium"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-201",
              "name": "Load balancer internet-facing",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Load balancer internet-facing"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A load balancer with internal absent or false is internet-facing (false is the provider default). Internet-facing LBs widen the attack surface and should be a deliberate choice.\n\nHow to fix:\nIf the load balancer must be internet-facing, attach a WAFv2 Web ACL and keep\nlisteners on HTTPS only. Otherwise make it internal:\nresource \"aws_lb\" \"this\" {\n  internal = true\n  # ...\n}"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/elasticloadbalancing/latest/application/application-load-balancers.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-203",
              "name": "Load balancer not dropping invalid headers",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Load balancer not dropping invalid headers"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Without drop_invalid_header_fields, malformed HTTP headers reach targets and enable request-smuggling style attacks. The provider default is false.\n\nHow to fix:\nReview the flagged configuration against AWS security best practices and the CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark. Apply the control the rule describes, preferring restrictive defaults, encryption, and audit logging. Validate with `aegis scan` after the change."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/securityhub-standards.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-207",
              "name": "RDS instance without backup retention",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "RDS instance without backup retention"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "With backup_retention_period absent or 0, automated backups are disabled and point-in-time recovery is impossible after data loss.\n\nHow to fix:\nEnable data protection. Turn on versioning / point-in-time recovery / automated backups with a retention period ≥ 7 days (30+ for production). Test restores regularly — untested backups are not backups."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/aws-backup/latest/devguide/whatisbackup.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "medium"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-307",
              "name": "Lambda function env vars without KMS",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Lambda function env vars without KMS"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "An environment block with variables but no kms_key_arn leaves environment variables (often secrets) encrypted only with an AWS-owned key.\n\nHow to fix:\nEncrypt Lambda environment variables with a customer-managed KMS key:\nresource \"aws_lambda_function\" \"this\" {\n  # ...\n  kms_key_arn = aws_kms_key.lambda.arn\n}\nBetter: don't put secrets in env vars at all — fetch from Secrets Manager at runtime."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/configuration-envvars-encryption.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-308",
              "name": "Lambda without active X-Ray tracing",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Lambda without active X-Ray tracing"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Without tracing_config { mode = \"Active\" }, Lambda invocations are not traced in X-Ray, reducing observability of failures and latency.\n\nHow to fix:\nReview the flagged configuration against AWS security best practices and the CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark. Apply the control the rule describes, preferring restrictive defaults, encryption, and audit logging. Validate with `aegis scan` after the change."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/securityhub-standards.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "medium"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-310",
              "name": "EC2 instance without detailed monitoring",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "EC2 instance without detailed monitoring"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "With monitoring absent or false (the provider default), the instance ships only basic 5-minute CloudWatch metrics, delaying detection of performance and availability issues.\n\nHow to fix:\nEnable audit logging. Turn on CloudTrail (multi-region, log file validation, KMS-encrypted bucket), VPC flow logs, and service access logs. Ship logs to a dedicated, MFA-delete-protected bucket in a security account."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/awscloudtrail/latest/userguide/cloudtrail-user-guide.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "medium"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-311",
              "name": "RDS without IAM database authentication",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "RDS without IAM database authentication"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "With iam_database_authentication_enabled absent or false, database access relies on long-lived passwords instead of short-lived IAM credentials, weakening credential hygiene.\n\nHow to fix:\nApply least privilege. Replace wildcard (`*`) actions and resources with the minimum scoped ARNs the workload needs. Split policy authoring from attachment. Use IAM Access Analyzer to generate scoped policies from CloudTrail history."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/best-practices.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "medium"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-323",
              "name": "Load balancer without deletion protection",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Load balancer without deletion protection"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Without enable_deletion_protection, a load balancer can be destroyed by a single API call or Terraform change, taking the service offline. The provider default is disabled.\n\nHow to fix:\nEnable deletion protection to prevent accidental destruction:\nresource \"aws_db_instance\" \"this\" {\n  deletion_protection = true\n}\nFor S3, remove `force_destroy = true`. For EC2, set `disable_api_termination = true`."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/USER_DeleteInstance.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "medium"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-336",
              "name": "CloudTrail not multi-region",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CloudTrail not multi-region"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A single-region trail misses API activity in every other region. is_multi_region_trail defaults to false.\n\nHow to fix:\nEnable CloudTrail in all regions with log file validation:\nresource \"aws_cloudtrail\" \"this\" {\n  is_multi_region_trail         = true\n  enable_log_file_validation    = true\n  kms_key_id                    = aws_kms_key.cloudtrail.arn\n  s3_bucket_name                = aws_s3_bucket.trail.id\n  include_global_service_events = true\n}"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/awscloudtrail/latest/userguide/cloudtrail-create-and-update-a-trail.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-339",
              "name": "Hardcoded credentials in resource config",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Hardcoded credentials in resource config"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A database password written as a literal in Terraform config is stored in state, plan output, and version control. The same holds for a secret-like variable or local flowing into a credential attribute. Reference a secrets manager or a generated password instead. The secret value is never included in findings.\n\nHow to fix:\nRemove the hardcoded credential and rotate it immediately — it is compromised.\nReference a Secrets Manager secret instead:\ndata \"aws_secretsmanager_secret_version\" \"db\" {\n  secret_id = aws_secretsmanager_secret.db.id\n}\n# then: password = jsondecode(data.aws_secretsmanager_secret_version.db.secret_string)[\"password\"]\nIf the value was committed to git, purge it from history (BFG/truffleHog) and rotate."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/secretsmanager/latest/userguide/intro.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-341",
              "name": "SNS topic without KMS encryption",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "SNS topic without KMS encryption"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "SNS topics should encrypt messages at rest with a KMS key. A topic with no kms_master_key_id relies on unencrypted delivery.\n\nHow to fix:\nEncrypt the SNS topic with a customer-managed KMS key:\nresource \"aws_sns_topic\" \"this\" {\n  kms_master_key_id = aws_kms_key.sns.arn\n}"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sns/latest/dg/sns-server-side-encryption.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-371",
              "name": "Load balancer without access logs",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Load balancer without access logs"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Access logs record every request to the load balancer and are essential for incident response and forensics. Enable them with an access_logs block pointing at an S3 bucket.\n\nHow to fix:\nEnable audit logging. Turn on CloudTrail (multi-region, log file validation, KMS-encrypted bucket), VPC flow logs, and service access logs. Ship logs to a dedicated, MFA-delete-protected bucket in a security account."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/awscloudtrail/latest/userguide/cloudtrail-user-guide.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "medium"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-443",
              "name": "S3 bucket policy does not enforce TLS (SecureTransport)",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "S3 bucket policy does not enforce TLS (SecureTransport)"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Without an explicit Deny on aws:SecureTransport=false, bucket objects can be fetched over plaintext HTTP. Add a bucket policy statement denying s3:* when SecureTransport is false.\n\nHow to fix:\nApply least privilege. Replace wildcard (`*`) actions and resources with the minimum scoped ARNs the workload needs. Split policy authoring from attachment. Use IAM Access Analyzer to generate scoped policies from CloudTrail history."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/best-practices.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-445",
              "name": "S3 bucket without server access logging",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "S3 bucket without server access logging"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Server access logging records requests against the bucket, which is essential for audit and incident response.\n\nHow to fix:\nEnable audit logging. Turn on CloudTrail (multi-region, log file validation, KMS-encrypted bucket), VPC flow logs, and service access logs. Ship logs to a dedicated, MFA-delete-protected bucket in a security account."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/awscloudtrail/latest/userguide/cloudtrail-user-guide.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "medium"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-446",
              "name": "RDS instance without Multi-AZ",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "RDS instance without Multi-AZ"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Single-AZ RDS instances have no standby for failover. Enable multi_az for production databases.\n\nHow to fix:\nEnable Multi-AZ for high availability and automatic failover:\nresource \"aws_db_instance\" \"this\" {\n  multi_az = true\n}"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/Concepts.MultiAZ.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "medium"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-506",
              "name": "Public load balancer without WAF",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Public load balancer without WAF"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "An internet-facing load balancer with no WAFv2 web ACL association is exposed to common web exploits, bots, and application-layer DDoS with no managed filtering in front of it.\n\nHow to fix:\nRemove public exposure. Restrict ingress to known CIDR ranges, move the resource into private subnets, and front it with a load balancer or API gateway if external access is required. Verify with `aws ec2 describe-security-groups`."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/userguide/vpc-security-groups.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-515",
              "name": "ECR repository without KMS encryption",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "ECR repository without KMS encryption"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "ECR repositories default to AES256-managed encryption. A customer managed KMS key (encryption_type = KMS) gives key control and audit. Absent configuration defaults to AES256.\n\nHow to fix:\nEnable encryption at rest. For AWS-managed keys add the service's encryption block (e.g. `storage_encrypted = true` for RDS, `server_side_encryption_configuration` for S3). For regulated data use a customer-managed KMS key via `kms_key_id` and enable key rotation."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/kms/latest/developerguide/concepts.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "medium"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-521",
              "name": "ECR repository allows mutable image tags",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "ECR repository allows mutable image tags"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "With image_tag_mutability = MUTABLE (the default when absent), an attacker who pushes to the repository can overwrite a trusted tag such as 'latest' with a malicious image. Set image_tag_mutability to IMMUTABLE so tags cannot be overwritten.\n\nHow to fix:\nReview the flagged configuration against AWS security best practices and the CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark. Apply the control the rule describes, preferring restrictive defaults, encryption, and audit logging. Validate with `aegis scan` after the change."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/securityhub-standards.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "medium"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-540",
              "name": "RDS MySQL/Postgres instance without IAM database authentication",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "RDS MySQL/Postgres instance without IAM database authentication"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "MySQL and Postgres RDS instances support IAM database authentication, which replaces long-lived passwords with short-lived IAM credentials. With iam_database_authentication_enabled absent or false, access relies on static passwords. Engine-scoped: only fires for mysql/postgres engines; aurora and unknown engines are out of scope.\n\nHow to fix:\nApply least privilege. Replace wildcard (`*`) actions and resources with the minimum scoped ARNs the workload needs. Split policy authoring from attachment. Use IAM Access Analyzer to generate scoped policies from CloudTrail history."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/best-practices.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-542",
              "name": "Lambda function not deployed in a VPC",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Lambda function not deployed in a VPC"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Lambda functions without a vpc_config block run outside your VPC, with no security-group or network-ACL control over their network access. Functions accessing VPC resources (RDS, ElastiCache) should declare vpc_config with subnet_ids and security_group_ids.\n\nHow to fix:\nReview the flagged configuration against AWS security best practices and the CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark. Apply the control the rule describes, preferring restrictive defaults, encryption, and audit logging. Validate with `aegis scan` after the change."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/securityhub-standards.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "low"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-543",
              "name": "RDS instance without CloudWatch log exports",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "RDS instance without CloudWatch log exports"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Hygiene: RDS instances should export database logs to CloudWatch via enabled_cloudwatch_logs_exports for auditing and troubleshooting. Without log exports, forensic analysis after an incident is limited.\n\nHow to fix:\nEnable audit logging. Turn on CloudTrail (multi-region, log file validation, KMS-encrypted bucket), VPC flow logs, and service access logs. Ship logs to a dedicated, MFA-delete-protected bucket in a security account."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/awscloudtrail/latest/userguide/cloudtrail-user-guide.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "low"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-544",
              "name": "RDS instance without auto minor version upgrades",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "RDS instance without auto minor version upgrades"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Hygiene: RDS instances should enable auto_minor_version_upgrade so security patches are applied automatically during maintenance windows. Without it, the engine can drift behind on security fixes.\n\nHow to fix:\nReview the flagged configuration against AWS security best practices and the CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark. Apply the control the rule describes, preferring restrictive defaults, encryption, and audit logging. Validate with `aegis scan` after the change."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/securityhub-standards.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "low"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-546",
              "name": "RDS instance without copy_tags_to_snapshot",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "RDS instance without copy_tags_to_snapshot"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Hygiene: RDS instances should copy tags to snapshots so backup resources inherit cost-allocation and ownership tags. Set copy_tags_to_snapshot = true.\n\nHow to fix:\nEnable data protection. Turn on versioning / point-in-time recovery / automated backups with a retention period ≥ 7 days (30+ for production). Test restores regularly — untested backups are not backups."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/aws-backup/latest/devguide/whatisbackup.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "low"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-547",
              "name": "Lambda function without X-Ray active tracing",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Lambda function without X-Ray active tracing"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Hygiene: Lambda functions should enable X-Ray active tracing for distributed request observability. Add a tracing_config block with mode = \"Active\".\n\nHow to fix:\nReview the flagged configuration against AWS security best practices and the CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark. Apply the control the rule describes, preferring restrictive defaults, encryption, and audit logging. Validate with `aegis scan` after the change."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/securityhub-standards.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "low"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-548",
              "name": "Lambda function without reserved concurrent executions",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Lambda function without reserved concurrent executions"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Hygiene: Lambda functions should set reserved_concurrent_executions to cap concurrency, preventing runaway scaling and unexpected cost. Set the attribute explicitly.\n\nHow to fix:\nReview the flagged configuration against AWS security best practices and the CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark. Apply the control the rule describes, preferring restrictive defaults, encryption, and audit logging. Validate with `aegis scan` after the change."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/securityhub-standards.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "low"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-563",
              "name": "Secrets Manager secret without customer-managed KMS key",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Secrets Manager secret without customer-managed KMS key"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Secrets Manager secrets should use a customer-managed KMS key (kms_key_id) instead of the default AWS-managed key, so key rotation and access policies are under your control. Set kms_key_id.\n\nHow to fix:\nEnable encryption at rest. For AWS-managed keys add the service's encryption block (e.g. `storage_encrypted = true` for RDS, `server_side_encryption_configuration` for S3). For regulated data use a customer-managed KMS key via `kms_key_id` and enable key rotation."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/kms/latest/developerguide/concepts.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "medium"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-567",
              "name": "Security Hub not enabled in module",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Security Hub not enabled in module"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "AWS Security Hub should be enabled for centralized security findings aggregation and CIS benchmark checks. Add an aws_securityhub_account resource. Only evaluated on modules with 5+ resources to avoid noise on trivial configurations.\n\nHow to fix:\nEnable Security Hub with the CIS and FSBP standards:\nresource \"aws_securityhub_account\" \"this\" {}\nresource \"aws_securityhub_standards_subscription\" \"cis\" {\n  standards_arn = \"arn:aws:securityhub:::ruleset/cis-aws-foundations-benchmark/v/1.4.0\"\n}"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/securityhub-settingup.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "low"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-568",
              "name": "CloudTrail trail without KMS encryption",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CloudTrail trail without KMS encryption"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "CloudTrail log files should be encrypted with a customer-managed KMS key so log integrity is under your control. Set kms_key_id on the aws_cloudtrail resource.\n\nHow to fix:\nEncrypt CloudTrail logs with a KMS key (see AEGIS-AWS-336 fix):\nresource \"aws_cloudtrail\" \"this\" {\n  kms_key_id = aws_kms_key.cloudtrail.arn\n}"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/awscloudtrail/latest/userguide/encrypting-cloudtrail-log-files-with-aws-kms.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "medium"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-569",
              "name": "Amazon Detective not enabled in module",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Amazon Detective not enabled in module"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Amazon Detective should be enabled for ML-driven security investigation across CloudTrail, VPC flow logs, and GuardDuty findings. Add an aws_detective_graph resource. Only evaluated on modules with 5+ resources to avoid noise on trivial configurations.\n\nHow to fix:\nReview the flagged configuration against AWS security best practices and the CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark. Apply the control the rule describes, preferring restrictive defaults, encryption, and audit logging. Validate with `aegis scan` after the change."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/securityhub-standards.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "low"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-601",
              "name": "S3 bucket without lifecycle configuration",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "S3 bucket without lifecycle configuration"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Hygiene: S3 buckets should define lifecycle rules to transition or expire objects, controlling long-term storage costs. Add a lifecycle_rule block or an aws_s3_bucket_lifecycle_configuration resource.\n\nHow to fix:\nReview the flagged configuration against AWS security best practices and the CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark. Apply the control the rule describes, preferring restrictive defaults, encryption, and audit logging. Validate with `aegis scan` after the change."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/securityhub-standards.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "low"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-604",
              "name": "Security group or rule without description",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Security group or rule without description"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Hygiene: Security groups and their rules should carry descriptions so reviewers understand their purpose. Add a description attribute.\n\nHow to fix:\nReview the flagged configuration against AWS security best practices and the CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark. Apply the control the rule describes, preferring restrictive defaults, encryption, and audit logging. Validate with `aegis scan` after the change."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/securityhub-standards.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "low"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-605",
              "name": "RDS instance without enhanced monitoring",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "RDS instance without enhanced monitoring"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Hygiene: RDS instances should enable enhanced monitoring (monitoring_interval \u003e 0) for OS-level metrics that aid troubleshooting. Set monitoring_interval to 1, 5, 10, 15, 30 or 60.\n\nHow to fix:\nEnable audit logging. Turn on CloudTrail (multi-region, log file validation, KMS-encrypted bucket), VPC flow logs, and service access logs. Ship logs to a dedicated, MFA-delete-protected bucket in a security account."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/awscloudtrail/latest/userguide/cloudtrail-user-guide.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "low"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-607",
              "name": "RDS Postgres instance without query logging",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "RDS Postgres instance without query logging"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Hygiene: RDS Postgres instances should export postgresql logs to CloudWatch for query auditing. Add \"postgresql\" to enabled_cloudwatch_logs_log_exports.\n\nHow to fix:\nEnable audit logging. Turn on CloudTrail (multi-region, log file validation, KMS-encrypted bucket), VPC flow logs, and service access logs. Ship logs to a dedicated, MFA-delete-protected bucket in a security account."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/awscloudtrail/latest/userguide/cloudtrail-user-guide.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "low"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-608",
              "name": "RDS instance without Performance Insights",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "RDS instance without Performance Insights"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Hygiene: RDS instances should enable Performance Insights for query-level performance analysis. Set performance_insights_enabled = true.\n\nHow to fix:\nReview the flagged configuration against AWS security best practices and the CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark. Apply the control the rule describes, preferring restrictive defaults, encryption, and audit logging. Validate with `aegis scan` after the change."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/securityhub-standards.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "low"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-609",
              "name": "Load balancer without access logging",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Load balancer without access logging"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Hygiene: Load balancers should log access to S3 for traffic analysis and incident investigation. Add an access_logs block with a bucket.\n\nHow to fix:\nEnable audit logging. Turn on CloudTrail (multi-region, log file validation, KMS-encrypted bucket), VPC flow logs, and service access logs. Ship logs to a dedicated, MFA-delete-protected bucket in a security account."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/awscloudtrail/latest/userguide/cloudtrail-user-guide.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "low"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-645",
              "name": "Lambda function without code signing",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Lambda function without code signing"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "An aws_lambda_function without a code_signing_config block deploys code without signature validation, so tampered deployment packages run unchecked. Enforce code signing with AWS Signer.\n\nHow to fix:\nReview the flagged configuration against AWS security best practices and the CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark. Apply the control the rule describes, preferring restrictive defaults, encryption, and audit logging. Validate with `aegis scan` after the change."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/securityhub-standards.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "low"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-647",
              "name": "Lambda function without dead-letter queue",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Lambda function without dead-letter queue"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Hygiene: an aws_lambda_function without a dead_letter_config block silently drops asynchronously-invoked events that fail all retries. Route failed events to an SQS queue or SNS topic.\n\nHow to fix:\nReview the flagged configuration against AWS security best practices and the CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark. Apply the control the rule describes, preferring restrictive defaults, encryption, and audit logging. Validate with `aegis scan` after the change."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/securityhub-standards.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "low"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-667",
              "name": "IAM role has no permissions boundary",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "IAM role has no permissions boundary"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The role sets no permissions_boundary, so nothing caps the maximum permissions its policies can grant — a single overly broad policy attachment becomes fully effective. Set a permissions boundary as a guardrail.\n\nHow to fix:\nApply least privilege. Replace wildcard (`*`) actions and resources with the minimum scoped ARNs the workload needs. Split policy authoring from attachment. Use IAM Access Analyzer to generate scoped policies from CloudTrail history."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/best-practices.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "low"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-673",
              "name": "IAM role has no description",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "IAM role has no description"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The IAM role has no description, so reviewers cannot tell what the role is for or who owns it. Document the role's purpose in the description field.\n\nHow to fix:\nApply least privilege. Replace wildcard (`*`) actions and resources with the minimum scoped ARNs the workload needs. Split policy authoring from attachment. Use IAM Access Analyzer to generate scoped policies from CloudTrail history."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/best-practices.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "low"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-677",
              "name": "IAM policy statement has no Sid",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "IAM policy statement has no Sid"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "At least one policy statement has no Sid, making the statement harder to reference in reviews, audit findings, and exception tracking. Give every statement a descriptive Sid.\n\nHow to fix:\nApply least privilege. Replace wildcard (`*`) actions and resources with the minimum scoped ARNs the workload needs. Split policy authoring from attachment. Use IAM Access Analyzer to generate scoped policies from CloudTrail history."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/best-practices.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "low"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-683",
              "name": "EC2 instance without termination protection",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "EC2 instance without termination protection"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "disable_api_termination is absent or false, so the instance can be terminated by an accidental or malicious API call. Enable it for instances that must not be deleted casually.\n\nHow to fix:\nEnable deletion protection to prevent accidental destruction:\nresource \"aws_db_instance\" \"this\" {\n  deletion_protection = true\n}\nFor S3, remove `force_destroy = true`. For EC2, set `disable_api_termination = true`."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/USER_DeleteInstance.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "low"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-684",
              "name": "EC2 instance without ebs_optimized declared",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "EC2 instance without ebs_optimized declared"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "ebs_optimized is absent, so the provider default (false) applies: the instance gets no dedicated EBS bandwidth. Declare ebs_optimized = true on EBS-backed workloads.\n\nHow to fix:\nReview the flagged configuration against AWS security best practices and the CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark. Apply the control the rule describes, preferring restrictive defaults, encryption, and audit logging. Validate with `aegis scan` after the change."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/securityhub-standards.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "low"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-692",
              "name": "EC2 instance without an IAM instance profile",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "EC2 instance without an IAM instance profile"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "iam_instance_profile is absent, so the instance has no IAM role: any AWS access must come from credentials baked into the image or user_data. Attach an instance profile with least-privilege permissions instead.\n\nHow to fix:\nApply least privilege. Replace wildcard (`*`) actions and resources with the minimum scoped ARNs the workload needs. Split policy authoring from attachment. Use IAM Access Analyzer to generate scoped policies from CloudTrail history."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/best-practices.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "low"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-696",
              "name": "EC2 root volume without encryption",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "EC2 root volume without encryption"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "root_block_device with encrypted absent or false leaves the instance's root volume unencrypted at rest, exposing the OS disk and any data written to it. Set encrypted = true.\n\nHow to fix:\nEnable encryption at rest. For AWS-managed keys add the service's encryption block (e.g. `storage_encrypted = true` for RDS, `server_side_encryption_configuration` for S3). For regulated data use a customer-managed KMS key via `kms_key_id` and enable key rotation."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/kms/latest/developerguide/concepts.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "high"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-699",
              "name": "EC2 instance without Nitro Enclaves enabled",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "EC2 instance without Nitro Enclaves enabled"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "enclave_options is absent, so Nitro Enclaves are unavailable on this instance. Workloads that process sensitive data should enable enclave_options { enabled = true } to isolate it.\n\nHow to fix:\nReview the flagged configuration against AWS security best practices and the CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark. Apply the control the rule describes, preferring restrictive defaults, encryption, and audit logging. Validate with `aegis scan` after the change."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/securityhub-standards.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "low"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-701",
              "name": "RDS instance without enhanced monitoring",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "RDS instance without enhanced monitoring"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "monitoring_interval is absent or 0, so enhanced monitoring is disabled: OS-level metrics that catch host problems are missing. Set monitoring_interval between 1 and 60 seconds.\n\nHow to fix:\nEnable audit logging. Turn on CloudTrail (multi-region, log file validation, KMS-encrypted bucket), VPC flow logs, and service access logs. Ship logs to a dedicated, MFA-delete-protected bucket in a security account."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/awscloudtrail/latest/userguide/cloudtrail-user-guide.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "low"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-702",
              "name": "RDS instance snapshots do not copy tags",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "RDS instance snapshots do not copy tags"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "copy_tags_to_snapshot is absent or false, so automated snapshots lose the instance's tags (owner, environment, cost center), which breaks tag-based access control and cost allocation on restores.\n\nHow to fix:\nEnable data protection. Turn on versioning / point-in-time recovery / automated backups with a retention period ≥ 7 days (30+ for production). Test restores regularly — untested backups are not backups."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/aws-backup/latest/devguide/whatisbackup.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "low"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-706",
              "name": "RDS instance on the default option group",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "RDS instance on the default option group"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "option_group_name is absent or a default group, so the instance runs with AWS's generic options (e.g. no OEM, no MariaDB audit plugin). Use a purpose-built option group.\n\nHow to fix:\nReview the flagged configuration against AWS security best practices and the CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark. Apply the control the rule describes, preferring restrictive defaults, encryption, and audit logging. Validate with `aegis scan` after the change."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/securityhub-standards.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "low"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-710",
              "name": "RDS master password not managed by Secrets Manager",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "RDS master password not managed by Secrets Manager"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A plaintext master password is set without manage_master_user_password, so the credential sits in state and is never rotated automatically. Use manage_master_user_password = true so RDS manages rotation in Secrets Manager. The password value is never included in findings.\n\nHow to fix:\nRemove the hardcoded secret immediately and rotate it. Store secrets in AWS Secrets Manager or SSM Parameter Store (SecureString) and reference them via data sources. Never commit secrets to version control — scan history with gitleaks if the value was ever committed."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/secretsmanager/latest/userguide/intro.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "medium"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-718",
              "name": "RDS instance without Performance Insights",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "RDS instance without Performance Insights"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "performance_insights_enabled is absent or false, so query-level telemetry is unavailable when investigating slow queries or suspicious database activity. Enable it for production instances.\n\nHow to fix:\nReview the flagged configuration against AWS security best practices and the CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark. Apply the control the rule describes, preferring restrictive defaults, encryption, and audit logging. Validate with `aegis scan` after the change."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/securityhub-standards.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "low"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-742",
              "name": "VPC without DNS hostnames",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "VPC without DNS hostnames"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "An aws_vpc with enable_dns_hostnames = false (the provider default) gives instances no public DNS hostnames, which breaks hostname-based service discovery and forces reliance on raw IPs. Enable DNS hostnames alongside DNS support.\n\nHow to fix:\nReview the flagged configuration against AWS security best practices and the CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark. Apply the control the rule describes, preferring restrictive defaults, encryption, and audit logging. Validate with `aegis scan` after the change."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/securityhub-standards.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "low"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-760",
              "name": "CloudTrail trail is not an organization trail",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CloudTrail trail is not an organization trail"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A trail that is not an organization trail records API activity for a single account only. An organization trail centralizes audit logging across every account in the organization.\n\nHow to fix:\nEnable audit logging. Turn on CloudTrail (multi-region, log file validation, KMS-encrypted bucket), VPC flow logs, and service access logs. Ship logs to a dedicated, MFA-delete-protected bucket in a security account."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/awscloudtrail/latest/userguide/cloudtrail-user-guide.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "low"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-761",
              "name": "CloudTrail trail without CloudTrail Insights",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CloudTrail trail without CloudTrail Insights"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "CloudTrail Insights detects unusual API activity and errors automatically. A trail with no insight_selector block never benefits from anomaly detection on management events.\n\nHow to fix:\nEnable audit logging. Turn on CloudTrail (multi-region, log file validation, KMS-encrypted bucket), VPC flow logs, and service access logs. Ship logs to a dedicated, MFA-delete-protected bucket in a security account."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/awscloudtrail/latest/userguide/cloudtrail-user-guide.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "low"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-773",
              "name": "SNS topic without display name",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "SNS topic without display name"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A topic with no display_name is harder to identify in notifications and audit trails. Set a human-readable display name for every topic.\n\nHow to fix:\nReview the flagged configuration against AWS security best practices and the CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark. Apply the control the rule describes, preferring restrictive defaults, encryption, and audit logging. Validate with `aegis scan` after the change."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/securityhub-standards.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "low"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-789",
              "name": "Secrets Manager secret without description",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Secrets Manager secret without description"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A secret with no description is unidentifiable in audits and secret inventories. Describe the secret's purpose and owner.\n\nHow to fix:\nRemove the hardcoded secret immediately and rotate it. Store secrets in AWS Secrets Manager or SSM Parameter Store (SecureString) and reference them via data sources. Never commit secrets to version control — scan history with gitleaks if the value was ever committed."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/secretsmanager/latest/userguide/intro.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "low"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-790",
              "name": "SSM SecureString parameter using default KMS key",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "SSM SecureString parameter using default KMS key"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A SecureString parameter with no key_id encrypts with the default aws/ssm key, which the account cannot rotate or scope via key policy. Point key_id at a customer-managed key.\n\nHow to fix:\nEnable encryption at rest. For AWS-managed keys add the service's encryption block (e.g. `storage_encrypted = true` for RDS, `server_side_encryption_configuration` for S3). For regulated data use a customer-managed KMS key via `kms_key_id` and enable key rotation."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/kms/latest/developerguide/concepts.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "low"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-836",
              "name": "Backup vault without vault lock configuration",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Backup vault without vault lock configuration"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A Backup vault with no aws_backup_vault_lock_configuration allows recovery points to be deleted before their retention period expires, undermining ransomware protection. Add a vault lock in compliance mode with a minimum retention.\n\nHow to fix:\nEnable data protection. Turn on versioning / point-in-time recovery / automated backups with a retention period ≥ 7 days (30+ for production). Test restores regularly — untested backups are not backups."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/aws-backup/latest/devguide/whatisbackup.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "low"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "AEGIS-AWS-844",
              "name": "API Gateway method without a request validator",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "API Gateway method without a request validator"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "An API Gateway method with no request_validator_id accepts unvalidated request bodies and parameters, pushing malformed input into the backend. Attach a request validator enforcing body and parameter validation.\n\nHow to fix:\nReview the flagged configuration against AWS security best practices and the CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark. Apply the control the rule describes, preferring restrictive defaults, encryption, and audit logging. Validate with `aegis scan` after the change."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/securityhub/latest/userguide/securityhub-standards.html",
              "properties": {
                "severity": "low"
              }
            }
          ]
        }
      },
